{key}VALVE CHANGES:{/key} Fibrosis + Calcification + Commissural fusion → Funnel-shaped valve

{key}HISTOPATHOLOGY:{/key} {hl}Aschoff's nodule{/hl}

{key}VALVE AREA THRESHOLDS:{/key}
• NORMAL: {ok}4-6 cm²{/ok}
• SYMPTOMS APPEAR: {warn}< 2 cm²{/warn}
• SEVERE STENOSIS: {hlpu}< 1 cm²{/hlpu}

{key}HEMODYNAMIC CONSEQUENCES:{/key}
↑ Left atrial pressure → Pulmonary venous congestion → Dyspnea
→ Left atrial dilation and hypertrophy
→ Atrial fibrillation develops
→ Pulmonary hypertension
→ Right ventricular hypertrophy → Right heart failure

{key}THROMBOEMBOLISM:{/key} Left atrial thrombus → Systemic embolization (Spleen, Brain, Periphery)

{key}INSPECTION:{/key}
• {hl}Mitral facies:{/hl} Dusky pink cheeks + upper lips (severe stenosis + pulmonary hypertension)
• JVD (late, with right heart failure)

{key}PALPATION:{/key}
• {hl}Tapping impulse:{/hl} Palpable S1 at apex
• {hl}Parasternal lift:{/hl} RV hypertrophy

{key}AUSCULTATION:{/key}
• {hl}Loud S1:{/hl} High LA pressure → Forceful valve closure
• {hl}Opening snap (OS):{/hl} Forceful valve opening
• {hl}Rumbling diastolic murmur:{/hl} Low-pitched, at apex, radiates to axilla, best heard with bell in left lateral decubitus
• {hl}Graham Steel murmur:{/hl} Severe pulmonary hypertension

{key}RIGHT HEART FAILURE SIGNS (Late):{/key}
Large V waves in JVP, Peripheral edema, Hepatomegaly, Ascites, Pleural effusion

{key}ECG:{/key}
• {hl}P mitrale:{/hl} Double-peaked P wave
• Right ventricular hypertrophy
• Atrial fibrillation

{key}CXR:{/key}
• {hl}Double shadow:{/hl} LA behind RA
• Straightening of left heart border
• LA enlargement, Valve calcification
• Pulmonary congestion

{key}ECHOCARDIOGRAPHY (TTE):{/key}
• Thickened/calcified leaflets
• Decreased mobility
• Decreased valve area
• Commissural fusion

{key}DOPPLER ECHO:{/key}
• ↑ Pressure gradient across mitral valve
• ↑ Pulmonary artery pressure

{key}TEE:{/key}
• {hl}Best for detecting LA thrombus{/hl}

{key}CARDIAC CATHETERIZATION:{/key}
• {hl}GOLD STANDARD{/hl} for severity assessment
• Measures: PA pressure, CO, Valve area

{key}CORONARY ARTERIOGRAPHY:{/key}
• For CAD assessment

{ok}MOST COMMON CAUSE: Rheumatic fever{/ok}

{ok}VALVE AREA:{/ok}
• Normal: 4-6 cm²
• Symptoms: < 2 cm²
• Severe: < 1 cm²

{ok}HISTOPATHOLOGY: Aschoff's nodule{/ok}

{ok}MOST PROMINENT SYMPTOM: Exertional dyspnea{/ok}

{ok}MITRAL FACIES: Dusky pink cheeks + upper lips (Severe stenosis + pulmonary hypertension){/ok}

{ok}TAPPING IMPULSE: Palpable S1 at apex{/ok}
{ok}PARASTERNAL LIFT: RV hypertrophy{/ok}

{ok}LOUD S1: High LA pressure → forceful valve closure{/ok}
{ok}OPENING SNAP: Forceful valve opening{/ok}
{ok}RUMBLING DIASTOLIC MURMUR: Low-pitched, at apex, radiates to axilla{/ok}
{ok}GRAHAM STEEL MURMUR: Severe pulmonary hypertension{/ok}

{ok}ECG: P mitrale (double-peaked P wave){/ok}
{ok}CXR: Double shadow (LA behind RA){/ok}

{ok}ATRIAL FIBRILLATION: Common complication{/ok}
{ok}SYSTEMIC EMBOLIZATION: From LA thrombus{/ok}

{ok}GOLD STANDARD for severity: Cardiac catheterization{/ok}
{ok}Test of choice for diagnosis: Echocardiography{/ok}
{ok}TEE: Best for detecting LA thrombus{/ok}

{key}MITRAL REGURGITATION (MR):{/key}
• Backflow of blood from {hl}LEFT VENTRICLE{/hl} to {hl}LEFT ATRIUM{/hl} during {hl}SYSTOLE{/hl}
• Caused by:
  - Pathology of mitral valve apparatus
  - Dilation of mitral valve ring
• Results in: Inadequate closure of mitral valve

{key}SYMPTOMS:{/key}
• Symptoms usually related to RAPID progression of disease
• Chronic MR shows symptoms similar to Mitral Stenosis
• BUT: {hl}Pulmonary edema is MORE COMMON in MR{/hl}

{key}SYMPTOMS (F-D-P-E):{/key}
• {hl}FATIGUE:{/hl} Due to decreased cardiac output
• {hl}DYSPNEA:{/hl} Due to pulmonary congestion
• {hl}PALPITATIONS:{/hl} Due to atrial fibrillation + increased stroke volume
• {hl}EDEMA and ASCITES:{/hl} Due to right heart failure

{ok}MR = Backflow of blood from LV → LA during SYSTOLE{/ok}

{ok}Caused by: Pathology of mitral valve apparatus + Dilation of mitral valve ring{/ok}

{ok}Pulmonary edema is MORE COMMON in MR than MS{/ok}

{ok}SYMPTOMS:{/ok}
• Fatigue (↓ Cardiac output)
• Dyspnea (Pulmonary congestion)
• Palpitations (AF + ↑ Stroke volume)
• Edema + Ascites (Right heart failure)

{ok}SIGNS:{/ok}
• Atrial fibrillation/flutter
• Cardiac enlargement + Displaced apex beat
• S3 gallop (LV dilation)
• Mid-diastolic murmur (↑ flow across valve)
• Crepitations + Pulmonary edema
• Dry cough + Prominent pulmonary S2
• JVD + Systemic congestion
• APICAL PANSYSTOLIC MURMUR = MOST IMPORTANT SIGN

{ok}ECG: LVH + LAH + AF + RVH (15%){/ok}

{key}AORTIC STENOSIS (AS):{/key}
• Failure of aortic valve to open adequately during {hl}LEFT VENTRICULAR SYSTOLE{/hl}
• Creates obstruction to {hl}LEFT VENTRICULAR OUTFLOW{/hl}

{key}LOCATIONS OF OBSTRUCTION:{/key}
• {hl}VALVULAR:{/hl} At valve level (most common)
• {hl}SUPRAVALVULAR:{/hl} Above valve
• {hl}SUBVALVULAR:{/hl} Below valve (from muscular hypertrophy)

{key}Most common cause:{/key} Calcific degeneration of a {hl}BICUSPID{/hl} valve

{key}NORMAL AORTIC VALVE:{/key}
• Three leaflets
• Orifice: {ok}3-4 cm²{/ok}
• Opens during systole → Blood ejected to aorta
• Closes during diastole → Prevents backflow

{key}IN AORTIC STENOSIS:{/key}
• Obstruction to LV outflow
• Sustained pressure gradient across valve
• Left ventricular {hl}PRESSURE OVERLOAD{/hl}
• {hl}Concentric LEFT VENTRICULAR HYPERTROPHY{/hl}

{key}CONSEQUENCES OF HYPERTROPHY:{/key}
• Relative myocardial ischemia
• Angina pectoris (even without CAD)
• Arrhythmias
• Left ventricular failure

{key}PROGRESSION:{/key}
• Early: Compensated (normal cardiac output maintained)
• Late: LV dilates + Wall thins → Systolic dysfunction
• During exercise: Cardiac output cannot increase adequately
• → Syncope, Arrhythmias, Sudden death

{warn}CRITICAL POINT:{/warn} When valve area ↓ to {hlpu}1/3 of normal{/hlpu}, symptoms appear; Without surgery, death occurs in {hlpu}2-3 years{/hlpu}

{key}HISTORY:{/key}
• Murmur since childhood → Congenital
• History of rheumatic fever → Rheumatic

{key}ASYMPTOMATIC (Mild-Moderate):{/key}
• For years, may be discovered incidentally

{key}CLASSIC TRIAD (Severe AS) - D-A-S:{/key}

1. {hl}EXERTIONAL DYSPNEA:{/hl}
   • Due to ↑ pressure in non-compliant LV

2. {hl}ANGINA PECTORIS:{/hl}
   • Due to ↑ O₂ demand by hypertrophied LV
   • Supply-demand mismatch (even without CAD)

3. {hl}SYNCOPE{/hl} (Temporary loss of consciousness):
   • During exertion
   • Due to inadequate cardiac output + ↓ BP

{key}OTHER SYMPTOMS:{/key}
• Acute pulmonary edema (possible)
• Sudden death (possible)

{key}PULSE:{/key}
• {hl}Slow-rising (parvus et tardus){/hl}
• Rapid fall in descending phase
• Best examined at {hl}CAROTID ARTERY{/hl}
• Narrow pulse pressure

{key}PALPATION:{/key}
• Apex beat: Usually normal position
• Sustained or double impulse (S4 or atrial kick)
• {hl}SYSTOLIC THRILL:{/hl} Palpable murmur at aortic area

{key}AUSCULTATION - MURMUR:{/key}
• Best heard at {hl}AORTIC AREA{/hl}
• Radiates to {hl}CAROTID ARTERIES{/hl}
• {hl}Harsh, crescendo-decrescendo{/hl} (saw-like)
• "Mew" sound in elderly
• Duration: Longer in severe obstruction
• {warn}INTENSITY does NOT correlate with severity{/warn}
• Very severe AS: Murmur may be {hlpu}INAUDIBLE{/hlpu}

{key}OTHER AUSCULTATORY FINDINGS:{/key}
• Systolic ejection click (if valve not calcified)
• Soft or inaudible S2 (if valve immobile/calcified)
• Reversed splitting of S2 (aortic component during expiration)
• Prominent S4

{key}SPECIAL CONSIDERATIONS IN ELDERLY (≥60 years):{/key}
• 20% have hypertension (50% moderate-severe)
• ↑ Arterial stiffness → Normal or rapid upstroke
• Pressure pulse may be wide
• Difficult to differentiate mild AS from aortic sclerosis (sclerosis = thickened but NOT stenotic)

{key}EARLY:{/key} May be {ok}NORMAL{/ok}

{key}LATE (PA view):{/key}
• {hl}POST-STENOTIC DILATATION{/hl} of ascending aorta
• Left ventricular enlargement

{key}LATERAL VIEW:{/key}
• {hl}Calcified aortic valve{/hl} (clearly visible)

{key}WITH HEART FAILURE:{/key}
• ↑ Cardio-thoracic ratio

{ok}AS = Obstruction to LEFT VENTRICULAR OUTFLOW during systole{/ok}

{ok}LOCATIONS: Valvular (most common), Supravalvular, Subvalvular{/ok}

{ok}CAUSES BY AGE:{/ok}
• Young: Congenital
• Young adults: Rheumatic + Bicuspid calcification
• Elderly: Calcific degeneration

{ok}Most common: Calcific degeneration of BICUSPID valve{/ok}

{ok}RISK FACTORS: Age, Male, ↑ LDL/Lp(a), HTN, DM, Smoking{/ok}

{ok}PATHOPHYSIOLOGY:{/ok}
• Pressure overload → Concentric LVH
• Relative ischemia → Angina (even without CAD)
• When valve area ↓ to 1/3 normal → Symptoms appear
• Without surgery: Death in 2-3 years after symptoms

{ok}CLASSIC TRIAD (Severe AS):{/ok}
1. Exertional Dyspnea
2. Angina Pectoris
3. Syncope (on exertion)

{ok}PULSE: Pulsus parvus et tardus (Slow-rising, low volume){/ok}
• Best examined at CAROTID ARTERY

{ok}MURMUR:{/ok}
• Harsh, crescendo-decrescendo systolic murmur
• Best at AORTIC AREA, radiates to CAROTIDS
• Intensity does NOT correlate with severity
• May be INAUDIBLE in very severe AS

{ok}OTHER SIGNS:{/ok}
• Systolic ejection click (non-calcified valve)
• Soft/inaudible S2 (calcified valve)
• Reversed S2 splitting
• Prominent S4

{ok}CXR:{/ok}
• Post-stenotic dilatation of ascending aorta
• LV enlargement
• Calcified valve (lateral view)
• ↑ Cardio-thoracic ratio (with HF)

{key}20% of all strokes are HEMORRHAGIC STROKES{/key}

{key}TYPES:{/key}
• {hl}INTRACEREBRAL HEMORRHAGE (ICH):{/hl} Blood in brain parenchyma
• {hl}SUBARACHNOID HEMORRHAGE (SAH):{/hl} Blood in subarachnoid space
• {hl}EPIDURAL HEMATOMA:{/hl} Blood between skull and dura
• {hl}SUBDURAL HEMATOMA:{/hl} Blood between dura and arachnoid

{key}SAH CAUSES:{/key}
• Ruptured intracranial {hl}ANEURYSM{/hl} (most common)
• Arteriovenous Malformation (AVM)
• Head trauma

{key}MOST IMPORTANT RISK FACTOR:{/key} {hl}HYPERTENSION{/hl}

{key}Epidural and Subdural hematomas:{/key} Due to HEAD TRAUMA, require SURGICAL treatment

{key}DEFINITION:{/key}
• Dangerous and fatal type of hemorrhage from skull trauma
• Due to rupture of {hl}MIDDLE MENINGEAL ARTERY{/hl}
• Artery located under the skull bone

{key}CRITICAL POINT:{/key}
• If treated {ok}QUICKLY{/ok} → Complete recovery without complications
• If diagnosis/treatment {hlpu}DELAYED{/hlpu} → {hlpu}DEATH is certain{/hlpu}

{key}CT FINDING:{/key} {hl}LENTIFORM (convex) shape{/hl}

{key}Same as ISCHEMIC STROKE + Specific measures for ↑ ICP{/key}

{key}MEASURES TO LOWER INTRACRANIAL PRESSURE:{/key}
1. Elevate head of bed
2. Cool the head
3. Hypertonic solutions:
   - Mannitol 20%
   - Hypertonic Sodium Chloride
4. Diuretics
5. Oxygen therapy
6. {warn}AVOID hypotonic solutions{/warn}

{key}CONTROL HYPERTENSION:{/key}
• Prevents re-bleeding

{ok}20% of all strokes are HEMORRHAGIC{/ok}

{ok}SAH CAUSES: Ruptured aneurysm (most common), AVM, Trauma{/ok}
{ok}MOST IMPORTANT RISK FACTOR: HYPERTENSION{/ok}

{ok}SAH STAGES (Hunt & Hess): I to V{/ok}
• Stage I: Mild headache + neck stiffness
• Stage V: Deep coma + Decerebrate rigidity

{ok}SAH DIAGNOSIS:{/ok}
• CT scan with contrast: Blood in subarachnoid space
• CSF: Bloody
• Four-vessel angiography: Finds aneurysm

{ok}SAH COMPLICATIONS:{/ok}
• Re-bleeding (HIGHEST RISK in 2nd week)
• Hydrocephalus
• Cerebral ischemia

{ok}SAH TREATMENT:{/ok}
• Medical: ICU, Bed rest 2 weeks, Laxatives, Nimodipine
• Surgical: Aneurysm clipping, VP shunt

{ok}EPIDURAL HEMATOMA:{/ok}
• Rupture of MIDDLE MENINGEAL ARTERY
• CT: LENTIFORM (convex) shape
• Early treatment = Complete recovery
• Delayed treatment = DEATH

{ok}ACUTE SUBDURAL HEMATOMA:{/ok}
• VENOUS source
• CT: CRESCENT (concave) shape
• 30% BILATERAL
• Suspect if symptoms worsen after trauma

{ok}CHRONIC SUBDURAL HEMATOMA:{/ok}
• Misleading disease → Presents as DEMENTIA or STROKE
• Elderly, Alcoholics, Parkinson's, Mentally retarded
• Often NO remembered trauma
• Symptoms appear days to 3 WEEKS after trauma
• Treatment: Surgery if ↑ ICP or progressive symptoms

{ok}ICP MANAGEMENT:{/ok}
• Elevate head, Cool head, Mannitol 20%, Hypertonic saline, Diuretics, Oxygen, AVOID hypotonic solutions

{ok}CONTROL HYPERTENSION prevents re-bleeding{/ok}
